---
[v1] net: ethernet: mtk_eth_soc: split mtk_ppe_start/stop handling
 from mtk_open/stop

In a WiFi extender scenario, all Ethernet ports may be brought down,
which previously caused the PPEs to be stopped as well. When a WiFi
STA tries to ping the DUT in this state, the PPE is unable to respond
with a feedback, preventing WED from forwarding packets to the CPU and
resulting in ping failures.

To address this, decouple the PPE lifecycle from the Ethernet port
open/close operations. After this change, the PPEs are kept active as
long as the Ethernet driver is loaded, ensuring that:
  - WiFi STA-to-DUT traffic can reach the CPU via the software path
  - WiFi-to-WiFi traffic can still benefit from PPE HNAT acceleration
even when all Ethernet ports are administratively down.
---
v1: Split mtk_ppe_start/stop handling from mtk_open/stop
---

From ef21367d6268aafdf9e8ed5c13e55cf1fe92811c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bo-Cun Chen <bc-bocun.chen@mediatek.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:39:18 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mtk_eth_soc: split mtk_ppe_start/stop handling
 from mtk_open/stop

In a WiFi extender scenario, all Ethernet ports may be brought down,
which previously caused the PPEs to be stopped as well. When a WiFi
STA tries to ping the DUT in this state, the PPE is unable to respond
with a feedback, preventing WED from forwarding packets to the CPU and
resulting in ping failures.

To address this, decouple the PPE lifecycle from the Ethernet port
open/close operations. After this change, the PPEs are kept active as
long as the Ethernet driver is loaded, ensuring that:
  - WiFi STA-to-DUT traffic can reach the CPU via the software path
  - WiFi-to-WiFi traffic can still benefit from PPE HNAT acceleration
even when all Ethernet ports are administratively down.

Signed-off-by: Bo-Cun Chen <bc-bocun.chen@mediatek.com>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c | 18 ++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
index a82bfab..95cfe45 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
@@ -4810,9 +4810,6 @@ static int mtk_open(struct net_device *dev)
 		if (err)
 			return err;
 
-		for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(eth->ppe); i++)
-			mtk_ppe_start(eth->ppe[i]);
-
 		for (i = 0; i < MTK_MAX_DEVS; i++) {
 			if (!eth->netdev[i])
 				continue;
@@ -4994,9 +4991,6 @@ static int mtk_stop(struct net_device *dev)
 
 	mtk_dma_free(eth);
 
-	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(eth->ppe); i++)
-		mtk_ppe_stop(eth->ppe[i]);
-
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -6124,6 +6118,9 @@ static void mtk_pending_work(struct work_struct *work)
 		__set_bit(i, &restart);
 	}
 
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(eth->ppe); i++)
+		mtk_ppe_stop(eth->ppe[i]);
+
 	usleep_range(15000, 16000);
 
 	if (eth->dev->pins)
@@ -6131,6 +6128,9 @@ static void mtk_pending_work(struct work_struct *work)
 				     eth->dev->pins->default_state);
 	mtk_hw_init(eth, true);
 
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(eth->ppe); i++)
+		mtk_ppe_start(eth->ppe[i]);
+
 	/* restart DMA and enable IRQs */
 	for (i = 0; i < MTK_MAX_DEVS; i++) {
 		if (!eth->netdev[i] || !test_bit(i, &restart))
@@ -7562,6 +7562,9 @@ static int mtk_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		err = mtk_ppe_internal_debugfs_init(eth);
 		if (err)
 			goto err_deinit_ppe;
+
+		for (i = 0; i < ppe_num; i++)
+			mtk_ppe_start(eth->ppe[i]);
 	}
 
 	/* we run 2 devices on the same DMA ring so we need a dummy device
@@ -7662,6 +7665,9 @@ static void mtk_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
 			mtk_usxgmii_pcs_put(mac->usxgmii_pcs);
 	}
 
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(eth->ppe); i++)
+		mtk_ppe_stop(eth->ppe[i]);
+
 	mtk_wed_exit();
 	mtk_hw_deinit(eth);
 
-- 
